vmhal9x (JHRobotics/vmhal9x 8bc480e) DestroySurface32 returned DDHAL_DRIVER_HANDLED for
surfaces DirectDraw's heap manager had allocated (DDHAL_PLEASEALLOC_*), so DirectDraw never
freed them: a released surface kept its video memory until the program's DirectDraw object
went away (T61 test: 32 x 256 KiB textures released, free memory unchanged). GTA 3 streams
textures, filled the 63 MB heap within minutes, and the Direct3D runtime then failed
thousands of texture creations a second (untextured world, very low frame rates; GTA 3 also
crashed on exit). Returning DDHAL_DRIVER_NOTHANDLED lets DirectDraw free the block.
Found 2026-11-17 with GTA 3 on the T61.

--- a/ddraw.c
+++ b/ddraw.c
@@ -408,7 +408,13 @@ DDENTRY_FPUSAVE(DestroySurface32, LPDDHAL_DESTROYSURFACEDATA, lpd)
 */
 	TOPIC("GARBAGE", "SurfaceDelete() success");
 	lpd->ddRVal = DD_OK;
-	return DDHAL_DRIVER_HANDLED;
+	/* NOTHANDLED: DirectDraw's heap manager allocated these surfaces
+	 * (DDHAL_PLEASEALLOC_*) and frees them only when the driver does not
+	 * claim the destroy. With HANDLED every released surface stayed allocated
+	 * until its DirectDraw object went away: GTA 3 streams textures, filled the
+	 * 63 MB heap within minutes and then failed thousands of texture creations
+	 * a second (untextured world, very low frame rates). */
+	return DDHAL_DRIVER_NOTHANDLED;
 }
 
 /* return tenths of millisecionds */
